  "response": "The origin of abrupt weakness is settled by combining the distribution of the deficit at the bedside with electrophysiological measurement, since the pattern of involvement already narrows the field before any instrument is applied. Myopathies characteristically cause symmetric proximal limb weakness, so that the patient struggles to rise from a chair, climb stairs, or lift the arms overhead. Peripheral neuropathies, by contrast, characteristically cause distal weakness accompanied by sensory loss, the feet and hands failing first while numbness or paraesthesia keeps company with the motor deficit.\n\nBecause nerve conduction studies measure the amplitude, latency, and conduction velocity of signals travelling along peripheral nerves, those recordings show that markedly slowed nerve conduction velocity indicates demyelination of the peripheral nerve. It is this demyelinating significance of markedly slowed nerve conduction velocity that brings a bedside sign about: as a result, reduced or absent deep tendon reflexes point toward a lesion of the peripheral nerve. Sensory nerve action potentials are abolished in pure disorders of the muscle fiber. That abolition cannot be squared with the hallmark status of sensory loss in peripheral neuropathy, for the sensory axons cannot both be left intact by disease confined to the muscle fiber, as that hallmark requires, and silenced by it.\n\nConduction testing is only the first stage of the electrodiagnostic study; the needle examination is undertaken afterwards, once those conduction measurements have been completed. Needle electromyography records the electrical activity of muscle fibers at rest and during voluntary contraction, and those tracings show that myopathic disease produces short-duration, low-amplitude, polyphasic motor unit potentials. By contrast, neurogenic disease produces large-amplitude, long-duration motor unit potentials, since surviving axons sprout to reinnervate orphaned fibres and so enlarge the territory of each motor unit. Fibrillation potentials on needle electromyography occur only in myopathy and never in neurogenic disease. Yet that confinement of fibrillation to muscle disease cannot be squared with the enlargement of the motor unit by reinnervation, because it is the loss of axonal supply that both leaves muscle fibres spontaneously active and remodels the unit into those large, long potentials.\n\nIn the case at hand, two outcomes of the survey for spontaneous activity exhaust the possibilities: either the needle examination showed no fibrillation potentials in any sampled muscle, or fibrillation potentials were present in at least one sampled muscle, and exactly one of these descriptions holds of the study, not both. The presence of fibrillation potentials in at least one sampled muscle indicates that the patient's motor nerve fibers were damaged. The patient's sensory nerve fibers were damaged. On the evidence assembled, injury had befallen at least one of the two fibre populations, the motor axons or the sensory axons, and perhaps both populations alike. The competing localisation is thus excluded not by preference but by the convergence of the pattern of weakness, the conduction data, and the needle findings.",
